@*
    One authored scalar, drawn as the value it is.

    `version`, an `alternatives` entry and every value of a key the schema does not
    define are this shape: a single word or number the author typed, with nothing
    behind it to follow. `knowledge-value` is the quiet treatment that says "this is
    the value" and promises no destination — a scalar dressed as a link would invite
    a click nothing is waiting for.

    A `code` element rather than a `span`, because these values are read
    literally: `10.0` is a version string and not the number ten, and the
    monospace face is what stops a reader rounding it.

    Prefix is how a value says which field it came from without a label in front of
    it. `v10.0` is a version anywhere it appears, and that is the whole of what the
    parameter is for: it is drawn as part of the value rather than beside it, so a
    reader who has the value and no row heading still knows what they are looking
    at. It is not a place to put a word — a prefix long enough to read as a label
    is a label, and belongs in the `dt`.

    Nothing in, nothing out. A blank scalar is a field the file did not state, and
    an empty value element would be a row asserting emptiness — which is a
    different claim from silence. A prefix alone is not a value: it is drawn only
    where there is something to prefix.
*@

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Value))
{
    <code class="@ValueCssClass" title="@Title" data-testid="@TestId" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">@Prefix@Value</code>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The value, exactly as authored. Never trimmed or reformatted: a
    /// stray capital in a <c>kind</c> is the author's, and normalising it here
    /// would hide the typo rather than its cause.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Value { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A sigil in front of the value, inside the same element — the
    /// <c>v</c> of <c>v10.0</c>. Part of the value on screen and part of the string
    /// a reader copies, which is the point: the prefix is what makes the value
    /// legible on its own.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Prefix { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The field this value came from, for a pointer user, where the row's
    /// own label is not on screen. Never the only place the field is named — the
    /// <c>dt</c> is, hidden or not — because a title is not reliably announced.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The class the value wears. Null drops the attribute, for a host
    /// laying values out under names of its own.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? ValueCssClass { get; set; } = "knowledge-value";

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }
}
